THREE COMPLAINTS, ONE SHAPE: the thing I was adjusting was downstream of the
thing causing it. Worth writing down together because I have now made this
mistake in three different subsystems in three consecutive passes.

  'glow is yellowy'  -> I brightened the white halo (0.16 -> 0.34) to out-shout
    the warm. The warm was FX:sparkles running on --particles = amber. Turning
    the source silver let the halo drop to 0.14 and resolved BOTH 'yellowy' and
    the 'way too bright' that my own fix had caused.
  'cards are purple'  -> I had recoloured experienceThemes (bars, titles,
    badges, dividers) to silver. Every card is wrapped in
    <BackgroundGradient palette='violet'>, which paints a ring OUTSIDE the
    border and is the widest-reaching colour on the card. A silver card in a
    violet halo is a purple card.
  'gradient hideous / no ash gray' -> I had answered an earlier 'low contrast'
    note by darkening the fill to slate-500. The contrast never needed to come
    from the fill; it comes from the highlight moving across it.

The general form: before tuning a property, find every layer that contributes
to the pixel being complained about. The one I own is rarely the only one, and
it is often not the dominant one.

ALSO — the 'once' tradeoff, resolved rather than accepted. scroll-reveal
defaulted to once:true for a real reason: a lingering CSS filter pins a
composited layer per section and a stack of those took this site to single-digit
fps. 'It should be recurring' looked like a straight conflict with that. It was
not: the cleanup only needed to key off VISIBILITY instead of finality. Strip
styles whenever an element settles in view, re-arm on exit — an on-screen
section carries no layer either way, and only the few scrolled out hold one.
Measured after: / 28, /portfolio 48, /more 55 fps, against a 27/40/42 baseline
A/B'd earlier this session. No regression.
When a request appears to conflict with an existing constraint, check whether
the constraint's ENFORCEMENT can move rather than assuming the tradeoff is real.

